{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And YHWH struck the people</b> — The verse did not state how many the dead were in the plague, as it stated the slain by the hand of the Levites, and as it specified the dead in the plague of Korah's dispute (Numbers 17:14) and that of Baal Peor (ibid. 25:9). Perhaps they did not die in a single plague, but YHWH struck them to die before their time, in the manner of what is written: 'For as YHWH lives, He will strike him, or his day will come' (I Samuel 26:10). Or perhaps He did not bother to count them, for likewise He did not count the dead in Taberah (Numbers 11:1) and in Kivrot HaTa'avah, of which it is stated (ibid. 11:33): 'And YHWH struck the people with a very great plague.' But those who fell by the hand of the Levites He specified, for their honor — to say that they killed many of the people and did not fear them, for they trusted in YHWH." "<b>And He said, 'because they made the calf'</b>: that they were not among those who prostrated to it and sacrificed to it, but they were among those who made it — that is, among those who gathered against Aaron and brought him the gold. And because He said that they were punished for the making of the calf, not for its worship, and they did not make it, He specified 'which Aaron made for them' at their command. And Onkelos (on Exodus 32:35) translated: 'because they served the calf that Aaron made' — he means to say that those who embraced and kissed it and desired it are the ones who were in the plague, and Onkelos does not give in his translation one act for two. And likewise, 'everything that was done there, he was the one doing it' (Genesis 39:22) he translated: 'and everything done there was done by his word.'\n\nNow this plague was after Moses killed the worshippers and prayed for them, and said, 'and if not, blot me out, I pray' — for because he offered his life for them, the Holy One, blessed be He, had compassion upon them, and said to him to bring them up to the land and that He would send an angel with them, and He wished to strike them somewhat for the great sin so that they would be worthy of this, and He sent this plague upon them — or He decreed thus upon them and the plague began. And after this He went back and said to him (Exodus 33:1): 'Go up from here, you and the people' — to say that the plague would not wipe away their sin enough for My dwelling in their midst, and He mentioned 'to the land that I swore to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob,' and said (Exodus 33:2): 'and I will drive out the Canaanite,' etc. — for because of the plague that He brought or decreed upon them, part of their sin was wiped away, and He was reconciled to them somewhat, to mention the merit of their fathers and that He would fulfill for them the oath to bring them to a good land, a land flowing with milk and honey, and He hinted to him that the land would not be corrupted and would not be defiled under its inhabitants because of their sin, and that He would drive out all six nations that were promised to them from the start. And He said by way of reconciliation (Exodus 33:3): <b>'for I will not go up in your midst'</b> — for your own good — <b>'lest I consume you on the way'</b> — because of your stubbornness.\n\nNow here Israel has two punishments: the first, that He would not cause His Shekhinah to dwell among them; and the second, that He would send an angel before Moses until He drives out the nations — but after they inherit the land, He did not promise them even an angel to help them, for this is why He specified 'on the way.' And over all this it says (Exodus 33:4): 'And the people heard this evil word and they mourned, and no man put his ornament upon him' — as mourners.\n\nNow HaShem is compassionate, full of compassion, and when He saw that they mourned, He went back and said in the way of mercy: 'Say to the children of Israel' (Exodus 33:5) — for until now He had mentioned 'your people' (Exodus 33:7 n.) and 'the people' (Exodus 33:1), but now He would mention them by their beloved name, and He commanded to tell them that it is for their own good that He would not go up in their midst, so that He would not consume them in an instant; rather, they did well to mourn and to grieve for their sin, and so they should do always, and 'I will know what I shall do to them' — that is, I will reckon their sin according to My knowledge, according to their mourning and their comfort over their iniquity, for I am the tester of hearts and the searcher of innermost parts.\n\nAnd by way of truth, 'and I will know what I shall do to you' will be in the knowledge of compassion that He will do with them, as in 'I will know' (Genesis 18:21). And I have already explained this (ibid., comm. on 20 'the cry of')."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The verse citations in this comment refer to Exodus 33:1–5, which Ramban quotes by phrase without explicit chapter-verse addresses; the parenthetical addresses shown were inserted by the Hebrew editor and are retained. The phrase 'זה הדבר' appears in the source at one point as 'הדבר הרע הזה' (Exodus 33:4), which is translated accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:56.849546659Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}